<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Amebic liver abscess (A06.4)</content>. Ultrasound: a single round 6.2 x 5.4 cm hypoechoic lesion in segment VII of the right lobe, without gas or a fluid level, with a small right pleural effusion. Entamoeba histolytica antibody positive; stool antigen and microscopy negative, which is common with a liver abscess; two blood cultures without growth after 48 hours. With fever, a tender liver, a single right-lobe abscess, positive serology and dysentery years ago in India, this is an amebic liver abscess rather than a pyogenic one. Dr. Raghunathan agrees. It is not large or in the left lobe and shows no sign of imminent rupture, so it does not need draining now. [S]<list><item>Metronidazole 750 mg three times a day with food for 10 days; no alcohol (he does not drink).</item><item>Then paromomycin 500 mg three times a day for 7 days, not at the same time as metronidazole.</item><item>Nurse phone check on the third day for the fever; aspiration only if fever persists after 72 hours.</item><item>Acetaminophen for fever and pain.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Positive interferon-gamma release assay (R76.12)</content>. IGRA positive. Chest radiograph: raised right hemidiaphragm with blunting at the right base from the abscess, and no consolidation, cavity or enlarged nodes. Three induced sputum smears and the NAAT on the first sample are negative. Active pulmonary tuberculosis is very unlikely, and his illness is explained by the abscess; he has tuberculosis infection without evidence of disease. Airborne precautions ended. Cultures will be final in six to eight weeks; latent infection is treated only after they are negative and the abscess is treated. [S]<list><item>Await mycobacterial cultures.</item><item>Discuss latent infection treatment at the six-week visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin B12 deficiency with anemia (D51.3)</content>. B12 168 pg/mL on a strict vegetarian diet, with hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL and MCV 99 fL; inflammation adds to the anemia. Folate 12.4 ng/mL. Ferritin 310 ng/mL is raised by inflammation. No neurologic signs. High-dose oral replacement works without injections. [S]<list><item>Vitamin B12 1,000 micrograms by mouth daily, long term.</item><item>Blood count at the end of the abscess treatment and at about eight weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 9 ng/mL: little winter sun and no fortified foods. He asked whether the tablets contain animal products; the pharmacist explained that cholecalciferol is made from lanolin in sheep&apos;s wool, that the tablet has no gelatin, and that a lichen-based form exists; he chose the standard tablet. [S]<list><item>Cholecalciferol 50,000 units weekly for 8 weeks, then 2,000 units daily.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Adjustment disorder with depressed mood (F43.21)</content>. He is relieved that the illness has a name and a treatment, and still low and lonely. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in his phone. He agreed to see the university counseling service. [S]<list><item>First counseling appointment this week, at a time he chose.</item><item>Dietitian visit next week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Temperature, symptoms and abdominal examination (liver tenderness, right lower rib tenderness)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>38.2 °C; tender liver edge 3 cm below the costal margin; right intercostal tenderness</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>51.8 kg, BMI 19.0</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Liver imaging</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat only for no response or a complication</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Single 6.2 x 5.4 cm right-lobe abscess; no drainage needed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Entamoeba histolytica serology, stool antigen and microscopy, blood cultures</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Serology positive; stool antigen and microscopy negative; blood cultures no growth at 48 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum acid-fast smears, NAAT and mycobacterial cultures</td><td>Three samples at baseline; cultures final at 6 to 8 weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three smears and the first NAAT negative; cultures pending</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Interferon-gamma release assay and chest radiograph</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>IGRA positive; radiograph without lung disease</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count and C-reactive protein</td><td>At diagnosis, at the end of the tissue course, then until normal</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: white cells 13.6, hemoglobin 11.2, platelets 412; CRP 92 mg/L</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (alkaline phosphatase, ALT, AST, bilirubin, albumin)</td><td>At diagnosis, at the end of therapy, and before and during rifampin</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: alkaline phosphatase 214, ALT 46 U/L, albumin 3.2 g/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Amebicide safety and completion (nausea, metallic taste, numbness on metronidazole; diarrhea on paromomycin; no overlap)</td><td>At the start and every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Side effects explained; metronidazole started today, paromomycin to follow and not overlap</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Response to vitamin B12 replacement</td><td>Reticulocyte count at 1 week; blood count at about 8 weeks; neurologic examination at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Neurologic examination normal; B12 started today; reticulocyte count due in one week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At diagnosis; after repletion</td><td>Up to date</td><td>9 ng/mL at intake; repletion started today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Diet and weight plan within his vegetarian rules</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dietitian visit booked for next week</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 and GAD-7</td><td>At least monthly while symptoms are active</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 12 and GAD-7 9 two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan and the 988 Lifeline in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Support far from home (counseling, contact with family and community, department and fellowship)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Counseling booked; letter for his fellowship given; international student office contacted with his agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Infectious diseases review</td><td>At diagnosis; then at six weeks for the cultures and latent infection</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Raghunathan today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A diagnosis within days, with tuberculosis either confirmed or excluded before any treatment that could hide it. In progress; target 2026-02-23 [S]</item><item>No fever, the abscess treated, and the luminal course completed. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item><item>Regain at least half of the lost weight within three months, on a diet that keeps his rules. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item><item>Vitamin B12 and vitamin D in the normal range and the anemia resolved. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 10 with no thoughts of self-harm, and regular contact with people who matter to him. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item><item>Complete four months of rifampin for latent tuberculosis infection. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Official American Thoracic Society/Infectious Diseases Society of America/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Clinical Practice Guidelines: Diagnosis of Tuberculosis in Adults and Children (2017); DPDx: Amebiasis (laboratory diagnosis, including antibody detection in extraintestinal disease) (2026); A Review of the Global Burden, New Diagnostics, and Current Therapeutics for Amebiasis (2018); Position of the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics: Vegetarian Diets (2016); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia and depression monographs (2026); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone call on the third day of metronidazole. Review in 12 days, at the end of metronidazole, with a blood count, CRP and liver tests. Call at once for severe abdominal pain, worse breathlessness, fainting or yellowing of the eyes. Next scheduled visit 2026-01-26: day 12: end of metronidazole and start of paromomycin, blood count, CRP and liver tests, mood and safety review. [S]</paragraph></text>

